@meta
id: 15-08
title: The cursor starts at the left edge
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-normal
par: 33
minutes: 6
vscode: find "should ", replace with nothing, hope it only hits test names
open: refund.spec.ts

@brief
The lint rule `jest/valid-title` has been switched on and it rejects
test names that begin with `should`. Every `it('should ...` in the spec
loses the word `should` and the space after it. Eight tests. Nothing
else in the file changes, including the `describe` and the comment that
happens to contain the same word.

This is a job for `:g//normal` with a motion in front of the edit,
because of one fact you need to know about how `:g` places the cursor.
On each marked line it puts the cursor in column one, the very left
edge, not on the first non-blank character. A `dw` there would eat the
indentation. Move first, then edit.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/normal keys` | replay `keys` on every matching line |
| `f'` | to the next single quote on the line |
| `l` | one character right |
| `dw` | delete the word under the cursor and the space after it |

@hints
The pattern should say more than `should`. The comment contains that
word too, and so does nothing you want to change. Include the quote
that comes before it.
--
On a matching line the cursor is at the left edge. `f'` gets it to the
opening quote, `l` steps onto the `s`, and `dw` takes `should ` in one
go.
--
`:g/'should /normal f'ldw` and Enter.

@solution
    :g/'should /normal f'ldw<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-nine keystrokes. Every one of the motion keys is from chapter 5
and chapter 8; the only new thing is knowing where the cursor starts.

Column one is a deliberate choice by vim, and it makes sense once you
see why: `:normal` is meant to behave the same on every line, and the
first column is the only position every line is guaranteed to have.
So a `:g//normal` sequence nearly always begins with a motion. `^` for
the first non-blank, `f` or `t` for a character, `$` for the end, `/`
for a pattern. Think of it as the address line of the edit.

The pattern is doing quiet work here too. `'should ` with the quote
matches the eight test names and nothing else; `should` alone would also
mark the comment on the `describe`, and on that line `f'ldw` would take
a bite out of the description. Pattern and keys are one design: the
pattern picks the lines, and the keys must be safe on every line it
picks.

@start refund.spec.ts
import { RefundProvider } from './refund.provider';

// These tests should run against the stubbed gateway only.
describe('RefundProvider', () => {
  it('should refund a shipped order in full', async () => {
    const result = await provider.refund('ord_4001');
    expect(result.amount).toBe(4999);
  });

  it('should refuse a refund on a cancelled order', async () => {
    await expect(provider.refund('ord_4004')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_CANCELLED');
  });

  it('should refuse a second refund', async () => {
    await provider.refund('ord_4001');
    await expect(provider.refund('ord_4001')).rejects.toThrow('ALREADY_REFUNDED');
  });

  it('should record the reason when the window has expired', async () => {
    const result = await provider.refund('ord_3001');
    expect(result.reason).toBe('REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED');
  });

  it('should not call the gateway when the window has expired', async () => {
    await provider.refund('ord_3001');
    expect(gateway.refund).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('should call the gateway once for a valid refund', async () => {
    await provider.refund('ord_4002');
    expect(gateway.refund).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  });

  it('should throw REFUND_FAILED when the gateway returns 502', async () => {
    gateway.refund.mockRejectedValue(new Error('502'));
    await expect(provider.refund('ord_4002')).rejects.toThrow('REFUND_FAILED');
  });

  it('should leave the order status alone on failure', async () => {
    gateway.refund.mockRejectedValue(new Error('502'));
    await provider.refund('ord_4002').catch(() => undefined);
    expect(orderRepository.update).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});

@expected refund.spec.ts
import { RefundProvider } from './refund.provider';

// These tests should run against the stubbed gateway only.
describe('RefundProvider', () => {
  it('refund a shipped order in full', async () => {
    const result = await provider.refund('ord_4001');
    expect(result.amount).toBe(4999);
  });

  it('refuse a refund on a cancelled order', async () => {
    await expect(provider.refund('ord_4004')).rejects.toThrow('ORDER_CANCELLED');
  });

  it('refuse a second refund', async () => {
    await provider.refund('ord_4001');
    await expect(provider.refund('ord_4001')).rejects.toThrow('ALREADY_REFUNDED');
  });

  it('record the reason when the window has expired', async () => {
    const result = await provider.refund('ord_3001');
    expect(result.reason).toBe('REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED');
  });

  it('not call the gateway when the window has expired', async () => {
    await provider.refund('ord_3001');
    expect(gateway.refund).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });

  it('call the gateway once for a valid refund', async () => {
    await provider.refund('ord_4002');
    expect(gateway.refund).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  });

  it('throw REFUND_FAILED when the gateway returns 502', async () => {
    gateway.refund.mockRejectedValue(new Error('502'));
    await expect(provider.refund('ord_4002')).rejects.toThrow('REFUND_FAILED');
  });

  it('leave the order status alone on failure', async () => {
    gateway.refund.mockRejectedValue(new Error('502'));
    await provider.refund('ord_4002').catch(() => undefined);
    expect(orderRepository.update).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
  });
});
